, cognitive psychology, and the emerging role of artificial intelligence in shaping knowledge production and enhancing our comprehension of cognition. Through a detailed examination of biological, cognitive, social, and cultural layers of the mind, Dr. Elkhalil demonstrates that true understanding cannot be limited to isolated models or laboratory experiments. The book addresses the central paradox of self-awareness—how the mind seeks to study itself—and offers methodological strategies for overcoming traditional epistemic limitations. Importantly, it highlights how artificial intelligence can act as a complementary cognitive partner, augmenting data analysis, pattern recognition, and mental modeling, while emphasizing that AI cannot replace the human mind’s capacity for meaning, intention, and value-based understanding. This foundational volume prepares readers for the rest of the \"New Consciousness\" series, including Mind Maps, Interactive Operating Systems, The Physics of Consciousness, and Artificial Consciousness. It serves as a vital resource for researchers, scholars, and curious readers seeking a modern, integrative epistemological perspective on human cognition and the evolving role of AI in scientific inquiry. Embark on an intellectual journey with Dr. Baroudi Elkhalil—from theoretical insights to the construction of precise mental frameworks, and toward a deeper understanding of how the mind and AI operate within the real world. “The New Consciousness Series” is an ambitious academic project challenging everything we know about the human mind across disciplines. Its goal is to renew our scientific heritage and reexamine centuries-old methodologies in mind and consciousness research. Born from the limitations and frustrations of current AI models, this series seeks to develop a new paradigm for understanding human cognition and creating artificial consciousness. Spanning seven volumes: Synthetic Epistemology – Foundations for a multi-level understanding 